    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "UK, France and China vote against UAE's Lana Nusseibeh at UN",
    summary: "UK, France and China vote against UAE's Lana Nusseibeh at UN Submitted by Oscar Rickett on Sat, 11/21/2026 - 07:50 Race for secretary-general job remains deadlocked, with Emirati diplomat vowing to continue despite opposition from three P5 countries UAE's Minister of State Lana Nusseibeh speaks at the United Nations headquarters in New York City, US, 27 October 2026 (David 'Dee' Delgado/Reuters) Off Lana Nusseibeh's bid to become the next secretary-general of the United Nations suffered a setback on Friday after the UK , France and China all voted against the Emirati diplomat in the latest informal straw poll conducted to help determine who should replace Antonio Guterres. Along with the US and Russia , the countries make up three of the P5, the permanent members of the UN Security Council that can veto any prospective candidate for the world body's top job. While votes in the straw poll are cast anonymously, diplomatic and other sources close to the Security Council told Middle East Eye that the UK, China and France were the P5 members who had voted to \"discourage\" Nusseibeh's candidacy. The US, Russia and Israel - which is not currently part of the Security Council and thus does not vote - all back Nusseibeh, according to the same sources. In total, the UAE minister of state and former ambassador to the UN received seven \"encourage\" votes, six \"discourage\" votes and two \"no opinion\" votes - the same result she achieved in the last straw poll, held on 6 November. Candidates need to secure at least nine votes to then put forward to the UN General Assembly, but in order for this to happen they need to have no \"discourage\" votes. British MPs urge government to block UAE's Nusseibeh from top UN job ebeca Grynspan, has ten encourage votes, but two discourage votes from members of the P5, believed to be Russia and the US. Argentina's Rafael Grossi, who is backed by his country's far-right president Javier Miliei and is favoured - like Nusseibeh - by the US, also picked up three discourage votes from members of the P5. With no agreement among the P5, the process remains deadlocked. Nusseibeh has vowed to stay in the race, posting on X: \"I remain fully committed to this campaign and will continue engaging Member States to earn their support and make the case for a more active, results-focused and responsive United Nations.\" Lobbying from the UAE The results remain a setback for the Abu Dhabi-born diplomat, who has been relentless in her pursuit of the job despite fierce opposition from civil society focused mainly on her country's involvement in the war in Sudan . In her official role in the UAE's government, Nusseibeh has denied reports from the UN Panel of Experts on Abu Dhabi's sponsorship of the Rapid Support Forces (RSF), a paramilitary force found to have committed genocide in Darfur by the UN and US . On Friday, Middle East Eye reported on efforts she had made to lobby Iain Duncan Smith, after the former leader of the UK's Conservative Party spoke out in parliament about the UAE's “sponsoring of a huge amount of terrorism in Sudan ” and called on the British government to use its Security Council veto to block her nomination. Exclusive: UAE's Nusseibeh lobbied UK's Iain Duncan Smith over UN candidacy ng with Nusseibeh he reiterated the position he had taken in parliament about the UAE’s involvement in Sudan - denied by Abu Dhabi - and called for the release of “two British citizens wrongly held in Dubai: Ryan Cornelius and Charles Ridley ”. Duncan Smith told Nusseibeh that the UAE needed to “cease and desist” from its involvement in the Sudan war. He told Nusseibeh that her UN candidacy gave the UAE a chance to do the right thing and withdraw from the war in Sudan and release Cornelius and Ridley, two British citizens who have been detained in Dubai for almost 20 years. “They are corrupt, and they are corrupting,” Duncan Smith said of the Emiratis, before calling on UK Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband to \"take a stand\". It would appear that this stand has been taken by Britain, as well as France and China. Nusseibeh and the UAE remain committed to her candidacy, though, and can use what one former British government official described to MEE as \"money and investment\" as leverage. Responding to MEE's request for comment on Friday, a spokesperson for Nusseibeh’s campaign said: “Lana Nusseibeh is meeting a wide range of stakeholders as part of her candidacy for UN secretary-general. \"We do not comment on the content of private meetings. Lana has taken a leave of absence from her ministerial role since the submission of her nomination. She took this decision as part of her genuine commitment to the independence of her campaign and potential future role.” United Nations News Post Date Override 0 Update Date Mon, 05/04/2020 - 21:19 Update Date Override 0",
